agent-docs/jusprin/mcp-tool-extension-guide.md:315:## Output design
agent-docs/jusprin/mcp-tool-extension-guide.md:328:A new tool that returns live state should include `sessionId` and `revision` when later calls need them to identify the project and to check that nothing has changed. `workspace_inspect` returns both, and returns the selection as ids. Results that are internal history records have their own schemas. MCP activity results also carry the action ID, the current session, and the current revision in `_meta["io.jusprin/activity"]`. When an event says that state changed, fetch a fresh snapshot. Never treat the event itself as the new state.
agent-docs/jusprin/mcp-tool-extension-guide.md:330:A successful MCP result contains `structuredContent` that is valid against the output schema, plus a text block that holds the same result serialized as text. An error uses the shared `{error: {code, message, details}}` envelope with `isError: true`. It does not use the success schema.
agent-docs/jusprin/mcp-tool-extension-guide.md:340:### Image results
agent-docs/jusprin/mcp-tool-extension-guide.md:344:- MCP: an `image` content block next to the text and the structured result. `2025-03-26` gets the same block, because image content is older than structured content.
agent-docs/jusprin/mcp-tool-extension-guide.md:349:## Errors should teach the next valid call
agent-docs/jusprin/mcp-tool-extension-guide.md:353:The MCP errors today include `invalid_arguments`, `cancelled`, `workspace_unavailable` and `stale_workspace`. The MCP adapter converts the app's native `stale_revision` and `stale_id` into `stale_workspace`, with the expected and the current session and revision in the details. It converts `unavailable_operation` into `workspace_unavailable`. Protocol errors at the transport level are separate JSON-RPC errors. Native error codes and MCP error codes do not have to be spelled the same.
agent-docs/jusprin/mcp-tool-extension-guide.md:366:## Read, mutation, and destructive policy
agent-docs/jusprin/mcp-tool-extension-guide.md:374:The action class is information used for UI wording, MCP annotations, staleness checks, and the audit history. A valid tool call starts as soon as it has been validated. A tool that previews changes counts as read-only only if tests against both the fake and the real workspace adapter prove that it does none of these: make a preset dirty, raise the workspace revision, create history, cancel slicing, or write a file.
agent-docs/jusprin/mcp-tool-extension-guide.md:376:## Asynchronous operations
agent-docs/jusprin/mcp-tool-extension-guide.md:388:MCP progress notifications are optional. They belong to one request and exist only to show progress. The progress value must never go backwards, and a client must always have another way to learn the final result. Add the MCP Tasks extension only when a real client or eval needs durable calls that outlive one request. An operation taking several seconds is not enough reason.
agent-docs/jusprin/mcp-tool-extension-guide.md:392:## Tool exposure
agent-docs/jusprin/mcp-tool-extension-guide.md:397:- Import from an attachment (`object_import`) is in-app only, and import from a path (`object_import_file`) is MCP only. In the first case the file reaches the app through the conversation. In the second it arrives as an explicit path. These are two separate definitions that each say exactly what they do, both built on one workspace command. They are not one tool that behaves differently for different callers.
agent-docs/jusprin/mcp-tool-extension-guide.md:398:- `intent_update` and `plan_set` are in-app only. The intent record and the pinned plan belong to the in-app chat. An external agent keeps what its user said, and its own plan, in its own conversation. MCP clients can still read both through `workspace_inspect` and `slice_report`. For the same reason, the in-app assistant's print guidance (`kPrintJourneyGuidance`), which names these tools, is not part of the MCP server's instructions.
agent-docs/jusprin/mcp-tool-extension-guide.md:399:- A future MCP diagnostics tool may be MCP only, if its purpose is to establish the external connection.
agent-docs/jusprin/mcp-tool-extension-guide.md:404:## When resources or skills are appropriate
agent-docs/jusprin/mcp-tool-extension-guide.md:406:Do not make anything depend on MCP resources until the clients we intend to support have shown that their models fetch resources reliably. A tool that returns a size-limited amount of data is what every client can be expected to handle. Resources may be added as an optional second way to get data that is large, readable, and stable.
agent-docs/jusprin/mcp-tool-extension-guide.md:408:Keep printing playbooks as skills the model can read (see [Skills versus tools](#skills-versus-tools)). Examples are reducing stringing, improving strength, and choosing a support strategy. Turn a workflow into an executable tool only when it corresponds to a named OrcaSlicer operation that always behaves the same way and has defined inputs, outputs, validation, and behavior for undo, reset, and cancellation. If many step-by-step workflows eventually qualify, build one workflow registry that the model can search, in preference to dozens of nearly identical tool definitions.
